.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt HALFINIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm halfinit
.Nd Lua userspace service manager and shadow startup planner
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Fl config Ar file
.Op Fl Fl target Ar name
.Op Fl Fl runtime-dir Ar directory
.Op Fl Fl state-file Ar file
.Op Fl Fl run-for Ar seconds
.Op Fl Fl activate-system
.Op Fl Fl check | Fl Fl graph | Fl Fl dry-run | Fl Fl shadow
.Op Fl Fl simulate Op Fl Fl scenario Ar name
.Op Fl Fl module-dir Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
loads declarative Lua services, validates their graph and supervises foreground
children using HalfBSD process descriptors and kqueue.
It runs as a userspace supervisor beneath the existing C-based PID 1.
HalfBSD defaults to Lua boot orchestration; traditional RC remains an explicit
loader recovery choice. Independent userspace test sessions remain supported.
.Pp
Without an execution mode or explicit configuration,
.Nm
rejects startup. The default system graph is for planning only.
.Fl Fl check , Fl Fl graph
and
.Fl Fl dry-run
report validation, parallel startup waves, reverse shutdown waves and missing
prerequisites as JSON without executing tasks or creating runtime files.
.Fl Fl shadow
also reads bounded rc.conf inputs for a comparison of known enable flags.
With a concrete boot policy it also reports kernel mount inventory and matching
external process names. It does not execute rc configuration or adopt processes.
.Pp
.Fl Fl simulate
uses disposable Lua fixtures in the actual scheduler and supervisor.
Scenarios are success, failure, recovery, exhaustion and forced.
Fixtures have independent lifetime bounds.
Select a private runtime directory when running unprivileged.
.Fl Fl run-for
sets an optional manager lifetime bound (at most 3600 seconds) for isolated tests;
normal managers remain persistent until shutdown.
.Pp
An explicit configuration file returns a table with services, target and optional
include (definition files), service_dirs, runtime_dir, state_file, log_file,
max_parallel and shutdown_timeout fields.
System definitions are under
.Pa /usr/lib/halfbsd/services ;
administrator files belong under
.Pa /etc/halfbsd
or an explicitly supplied private path.
No administrator configuration is overwritten by installworld.
.Pp
The runtime directory must be owned by the manager UID and not group/world
writable. An advisory lock prevents a second manager using the same directory.
The control socket is
.Pa control.sock ;
enabled preferences are written atomically to
.Pa enabled.json
unless an alternate state file is supplied.
.Sh SERVICES
Services have a name, description, type, requires, wants, after, before and
optional process/credential/readiness/restart attributes.
Definitions load in a restricted declarative environment without OS, process,
IO or module-loading operations.
.Bl -tag -width oneshot
.It Sy daemon
A foreground process, supervised until stopped or failed.
.It Sy oneshot
A command becoming ready after exit zero, or an explicit native action provider.
.It Sy target
A group without its own process.
.It Sy virtual
A bounded registered condition provider, without its own process.
.El
.Pp
Requires activates dependencies and gates on READY. Failure stops active hard
dependents; they can resume after recovery. Wants activates optional dependencies
and waits for them to settle, without propagating their failure.
After/before add ordering only if both services are selected.
All combined dependency/ordering cycles are rejected before startup.
Independent eligible services start concurrently, subject to max_parallel.
.Pp
Readiness may be process, notify or condition. Process means successful exec
only and is not proof of daemon initialization. Notify provides a descriptor
number in HALFINIT_READY_FD; the child writes exactly READY followed by newline.
Condition invokes a registered provider through bounded kernel-timer retries.
Start_timeout bounds readiness; stop_timeout bounds TERM grace.
.Pp
Standard input is null. Standard output/error default to null; stdout/stderr
may specify absolute file paths, opened append-only through the native process
API. Manager lifecycle logs remain separate structured records.
.Pp
Restart policies are never, on-failure and always. Restart_limit/window bound
retry bursts. Delay grows exponentially to restart_max_delay. Exhaustion is
terminal until an explicit retry/restart. Readiness failures count as failures.
.Pp
Lifecycle states are INACTIVE, QUEUED, STARTING, READY, STOPPING, FAILED,
RESTARTING and SKIPPED. Every transition records monotonic time, previous/new
state and reason; history is bounded. Logs use halfbsd.log structured records.
.Sh CONTROL AND SHUTDOWN
TERM and INT request reverse dependency shutdown. Independent services within a
wave stop together. TERM grace is followed by KILL and native reaping. An overall
shutdown deadline and final five-second reap bound prevent indefinite waiting.
HUP requests configuration reload.
.Pp
Reload validates the entire candidate before committing it. Active service
removal/type/dependency changes are rejected; stop them first. Other changed
launch attributes apply at the next launch, while restart policy applies to
subsequent failures. Runtime/socket/state/log relocation is rejected.
Changing the initial target during reload is rejected; use explicit target
start/stop commands or restart the manager with the new target.
Invalid candidates preserve the live registry.
.Sh SECURITY
The Unix socket authenticates FreeBSD peer credentials. Read-only commands are
available to clients which can reach it; mutations require manager UID or root.
Requests are versioned JSON, size-bounded and never evaluated as Lua or shell.
Connections and their lifetimes are bounded.
.Pp
System services require an explicit configuration and
.Fl Fl activate-system .
The built-in legacy graph remains a planning outline. A configuration with
.Sy boot
compiles a concrete graph from explicit administrator policy.
Neither installation nor any planning mode activates it.
Existing daemons outside this manager cause preflight refusal; never use live
activation to take over a running rc host.
.Pp
Foreground execution is required. Escaped or daemonized descendants are not
owned reliably after leader reaping; stale process groups are never signaled.
.Sh BOOT ADAPTERS
The installed examples
.Pa /usr/share/examples/halfbsd/boot-system.lua
and
.Pa /usr/share/examples/halfbsd/desktop-user.lua
show system and existing-login-session policies.
System boot fields are mode, interfaces, local_mounts, zfs, root_writable,
firewall, dns, sshd, ntpd, syslogd, seatd, desktop, swap, device_rules and hostname.
Root privilege and exclusive process ownership are checked before mutation.
.Pp
Storage preparation runs through the existing scheduler before the manager
opens its runtime lock, control socket, persistent state or optional file log.
Console logging is available during that preparation.
Local mounts can be selected from fstab or supplied as native nmount option
records. Root, swap, ZFS and noauto entries are excluded from the fstab list.
Unsupported or remote filesystems require explicit separate policy.
Nested mounts depend on their parents. Mounted filesystems are not checked or
adopted for cleanup. Unmounted UFS and FAT entries can use fsck preen.
ZFS mounts already imported pools; pool imports, encryption keys and exports
remain outside this adapter.
.Pp
Created local mounts are released in reverse dependency order after process
cleanup and runtime descriptors close. There is no forced unmount or blanket
ZFS unmount. Kernel filesystem operations are synchronous and are not made
interruptible by the manager's timers.
.Pp
Interfaces may be explicitly selected or one Ethernet interface chosen with
carrier preference and stable name ordering. Native ioctl brings it up; the
existing foreground dhclient supplies addresses and routes. Readiness requires
an IPv4 address and default route. DHCP DNS addresses remain ignored by the
existing HalfBSD resolver integration.
.Pp
Firewall ownership defaults to verify: kernel hooks, an immutable set 31 deny
guard and default deny must be present. Explicit load requires an empty deny
baseline; it installs the guard before rules and never flushes existing policy.
Workstation, closed and open modes are explicit. Workstation permits SSH and
DNS from any source, matching the established HalfBSD policy. Every installed
rule is read back before externally reachable services start.
.Pp
DNS uses local-unbound-checkconf, local-unbound-anchor and foreground
local-unbound. Existing valid anchors are preserved; initialization is run as
unbound when needed. Readiness requires a successful matching localhost UDP
answer and a TCP connection. Existing DNSSEC maintenance and the compiled DNS
provider hierarchy remain in Unbound. SSH keys are generated idempotently,
configuration is checked, and readiness probes TCP. NTP runs foreground; its
process readiness does not claim clock synchronization.
.Pp
Seatd is a required base workstation service, started from
.Pa /usr/sbin/seatd
with a root:video socket mode 0770 at
.Pa /var/run/seatd.sock .
Workstation users need explicit video group membership.
Multiuser waits for socket ownership, permissions and connection readiness.
No seatd=true setting is needed. An old boolean boot.seatd setting is normalized
with a warning without rewriting administrator configuration; it cannot disable
the required service. Diagnostic network/single targets still omit seatd.
Use halfsvc to administer the Lua-owned daemon; do not start a duplicate ports
instance. The base libseat client API, shared/static library and pkg-config
metadata are installed with world.
.Pp
The desktop user policy requires a private XDG runtime directory and current
foreground login terminal, with terminal TOSTOP disabled for console logging.
A tracked child receives terminal ownership and
SIGCONT through its process descriptor; exit or shutdown restores the manager's
own living group. Sway readiness defaults to the Wayland socket named
.Pa wayland-0
under that runtime directory; niri requires an explicit absolute
.Sy wayland_socket .
A pre-existing readiness socket causes refusal.
The system desktop target can wait for the configured non-root user's manager
socket reporting a READY compositor; it does not adopt that user's processes.
Console autologin, PAM and persistent session ownership are future work.
.Pp
Boot policy cannot change during configuration reload; start a new manager
session to change it. Gateway is an explicit service group, without inferred
packet forwarding or NAT configuration. The current diagnostic boot stage is
described below.
.Sh BOOT INTEGRATION
The current default is full Lua multiuser startup:
.Sy halfbsd.boot_stage=multiuser .
It waits for configured DNS, SSH, NTP and other required services before PID 1
starts authenticated console logins. The supervisor remains alive.
The kernel handoff result is
.Sy halfbsd.boot_result=multiuser-ready .
Startup remains serial under the installed example configuration.
.Pp
The earlier network inspection checkpoint remains explicitly selectable with
.Sy halfbsd.boot_stage=network .
It remounts root writable, initializes configured local storage, AMDGPU,
runtime directories, swap, hostname, library hints, local logging, IPFW and DHCP.
It waits for an address and default route, then opens the existing PID 1
single-user shell while halfinit retains ownership of DHCP and logging.
DNS, SSH, NTP, desktop services and gettys are not started.
The control interface permits only list, status and graph at this checkpoint.
Use normal reboot for ordered cleanup; exiting the shell does not advance boot.
.Pp
Expect kernel variables halfbsd.boot_active=lua, halfbsd.boot_stage=network
and halfbsd.boot_result=network-ready.
An explicit loader override halfbsd.boot_mode=rc still selects recovery.
The earlier minimal diagnostic remains available with
.Sy halfbsd.boot_stage=single ;
the following describes that explicit diagnostic, not the multiuser default.
PID 1 prints a dated diagnostic-build banner and selected startup path directly
to the console. The native launcher and Lua print unbuffered progress; PID 1
prints a waiting message every five seconds while Lua runs.
.Pp
The diagnostic stage verifies the kernel-mounted ZFS root and devfs, invokes
.Pa /sbin/zfs mount -a
for already-imported datasets, then runs
.Pa /sbin/kldload -n -v amdgpu
and
.Pa /sbin/kldstat -n amdgpu.ko .
A DRM card device must appear. This proves neither display acceleration nor
compositor operation. AMDGPU and its firmware must already be installed and
compatible with the running kernel. No driver package is installed automatically.
.Pp
The diagnostic path bypasses
.Pa /etc/halfbsd/system.lua
and enabled-state files entirely. It starts no network interfaces, DHCP, firewall
policy loader, DNS, SSH, NTP, syslog, swap, getty or desktop services.
It does not import pools, remount root writable or mount fstab filesystems.
ZFS mount changes and the loaded graphics driver are retained for shell inspection.
After a distinct SINGLE notification and successful Lua process exit, PID 1
opens its existing single-user shell. Errors print their reason and open recovery
instead. Neither case falls back automatically to RC.
.Pp
At the loader prompt select the diagnostic test explicitly with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
set halfbsd.boot_mode=lua
set halfbsd.boot_stage=single
boot
.Ed
Do not use boot -s for the test: that requests PID 1's direct single-user shell
before the Lua handoff. Once at the diagnostic shell, inspect:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kenv halfbsd.boot_active
kenv halfbsd.boot_stage
kenv halfbsd.boot_result
mount -t zfs
kldstat -n amdgpu.ko
ls /dev/dri
.Ed
The expected kernel values are lua, single and single-ready. A shell by itself
is not proof of Lua success. The diagnostic Lua process exits before the shell,
so no persistent halfinit process or halfsvc socket is expected.
Exiting that shell stays single-user; it does not activate networking.
.Pp
Explicit loader
.Sy halfbsd.boot_mode=rc
retains traditional RC recovery.
.Sy halfbsd.boot_stage=multiuser
selects the default full Lua service-manager path described below.
A mode/stage change after initialization requires rebooting; no partially
initialized RC/Lua switching is attempted.
.Pp
The following describes the default FULL MULTIUSER path. Lua is the default startup system; no marker file is required.

The C-based PID 1 retains its login, single-user and kernel reboot responsibilities.
Initially supported boot layouts require a kernel-mounted ZFS root containing
the interpreter, manager, modules and their libraries; a separately mounted
.Pa /usr
is not supported by this early integration.
.Pp
Installation seeds a root-owned mode-0600
.Pa /etc/halfbsd/system.lua
from the installed workstation example only when that path is absent.
Existing administrator contents, permissions and symlinks are never replaced.
Unsafe configuration ownership or symlinks are rejected at boot.
The default selects DHCP Ethernet discovery, filesystem and swap initialization,
IPFW workstation policy, Unbound, SSH, NTP, local-only foreground syslogd,
shared-library hints and authenticated console login.
A first live install preserves the current valid ASCII kernel hostname; staged
images default to halfbsd. Verify the intended site hostname before reboot.
Review custom filesystem, device and network requirements before the first boot.
.Pp
The loader variable
.Sy halfbsd.boot_mode=rc
selects traditional RC for one boot; setting it to
.Sy lua
explicitly selects Lua.
Use the loader prompt rather than loader.conf for a one-boot override.
Invalid selection enters single-user recovery. The former opt-in marker is
obsolete and has no effect.
.Pp
PID 1 launches the manager with an internal descriptor-3 readiness channel.
That option requires root with PID 1 as its direct parent.
After devfs, runtime storage, loopback, firewall, control and the complete
selected multiuser target are ready, PID 1 starts existing console login sessions
while the manager continues supervising. Configured swap is a hard requirement.
Failure of required network or service startup enters single-user recovery,
rather than announcing an incomplete multiuser environment.
The legacy rc.console terminal entry becomes
.Xr halfconsole 8
in Lua mode, without invoking that shell helper.
Local console policy defaults to root autologin with a three-second account
selector; set console.autologin=false for authenticated login.
Other getty sessions and SSH authentication remain unchanged.
.Pp
The top-level profile selects workstation (default), network or maintenance.
Workstation starts the configured multiuser target, including mandatory devd
and seatd. Network selects DHCP and the inspection graph, omitting downstream
services. Maintenance selects filesystems, graphics, runtime, loopback, swap
and firewall without DHCP or network services. Diagnostic profiles discard
custom service includes and persisted activations and allow only read-only
control. They apply on a fresh boot, not as live session isolation mechanisms.
Configuration-selected profiles use normal console login after readiness;
the loader boot_stage=network remains the dedicated inspection-shell entry.
Independent PID 1 recovery and explicit RC mode remain available.
.Pp
The kernel/PID 1 devfs mount is verified rather than duplicated.
Temporary directories are prepared with mode 1777 and runtime storage with 0755.
.Sy device_rules
is an explicit array of path, mode and optional uid/gid records for real devfs
entries; it does not execute rc devfs scripts or discover arbitrary policy.
.Sy swap
accepts false, fstab (the default), or an array of existing /dev paths.
No swap file, encrypted device attachment or pool import is synthesized.
Already-active swap is not adopted for cleanup; only newly activated devices
are deactivated.
.Sy hostname
optionally sets the host name through the native API.
.Pp
Ordinary shutdown and reboot request PID 1's ordered Lua shutdown.
The manager refuses new mutations, stops dependents first, releases owned
resources and synchronizes filesystems. PID 1 then performs its established
remaining-process cleanup and kernel reboot. Lua mode executes neither
rc.shutdown nor rc.final. Explicit quick reboot bypasses orderly cleanup;
advanced reboot flags and live reroot are outside the initial integration.
.Pp
Real PID 1 sessions save bounded persistent shutdown snapshots in
.Pa /var/db/halfbsd .
After reboot, run halfsvc shutdown-report as root to compare Lua resource
cleanup with PID 1's observed supervisor exit. Reports have boot identities
so an older successful shutdown is not attributed to a newer recorded boot.
Reporting never holds storage open across cleanup; unavailable or unmounted
reporting storage leaves incomplete evidence rather than a success claim.
.Pp
Unexpected manager exit or critical startup failure enters single-user recovery,
without automatically running RC over partially initialized Lua state.
Once startup has been attempted, changing startup systems in the same boot is
refused. Reboot and select RC at the loader prompt.
Preserve console access, a recoverable VM disk and traditional RC throughout
validation; installation alone does not establish live boot acceptance. This cutover remains
experimental until actual Lua-controlled boot and recovery have been tested.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr halfsvc 8 ,
.Xr lua-halfbsd 7
